== utils.extmath
ok   randomized_svd on an exactly rank-5 60x40 matrix: singular values = exact SVD (rel 1e-10), U S Vt reconstructs M  (max rel 5.9e-16)
ok   randomized_svd(flip_sign=True) (60, 40): the largest-|.| loading of each left singular vector is positive
ok   randomized_svd on an exactly rank-5 30x70 matrix: singular values = exact SVD (rel 1e-10), U S Vt reconstructs M  (max rel 2.7e-16)
ok   randomized_svd(flip_sign=True) (30, 70): the largest-|.| loading of each left singular vector is positive
ok   randomized_svd on a full-rank Gaussian matrix, n_iter=10: top-10 singular values within 2% of exact  (max rel 2.5e-15)
ok   weighted_mode docstring example 1: ([4,1,4,2,4,2], ones) -> (array([4.]), array([3.]))
ok   weighted_mode docstring example 2: weights [1,3,0.5,1.5,1,2] -> (array([2.]), array([3.5]))
ok   weighted_mode tie ('more than one such value, only the first is returned'): smallest tied value
ok   weighted_mode(axis=1) on a 2-D array = per-row weighted mode (plain Python)
ok   safe_sparse_dot(sparse, dense) = dense product (returned dense)
ok   safe_sparse_dot(sparse, sparse) is sparse unless dense_output=True
ok   safe_sparse_dot(dense 1-D, dense 1-D) = inner product
ok   row_norms = sqrt(sum x^2) per row; squared=True the squares; sparse same
ok   stable_cumsum: float64 cumulative sum of the float32 input
ok   softmax = exp(x) / sum exp(x) per row (mpmath), rows sum to 1, no overflow for |x| ~ 100
ok   softmax(1000 + x) = softmax(x) (max subtracted before exp)
ok   softmax(copy=False) works in place
ok   density(sparse) = nnz / (rows * cols) (docstring example: 0.25)
ok   cartesian docstring example (([1,2,3],[4,5],[6,7])) -> 12 rows in lexicographic order
ok   fast_logdet docstring example [[5,1],[2,8]] -> log(38)
ok   fast_logdet(SPD) = log det (mpmath)
ok   fast_logdet: negative determinant -> -inf; singular -> -inf
